      "intro": "If your loved one is struggling to afford fresh fruits and vegetables on a fixed income, New Mexico's Senior Farmers' Market Nutrition Program provides up to $100 per year (2024) on an electronic card to shop at local farmers markets and roadside stands. The program targets seniors with household income at or below 185% of federal poverty level, $2,739/month for a single person in 2024.\n\nBecause this program operates on a first-come, first-served basis and typically opens applications in mid-February, timing matters. Your loved one must be 60+ (or 55+ if Native American) and reapply annually even if they participated before. The electronic card works only for locally grown fresh fruits, vegetables, herbs, and honey at authorized New Mexico farmers and markets.\n\nBecause funding is limited each year, the program may close once allocations are exhausted. Benefits are distributed in July to approved applicants, so early application in February-March gives your loved one the best chance of receiving assistance.",
      "savingsRange": "Up to $50 per eligible senior for the 2026 season (state funds, when available, can raise this to up to $100)",
      "savingsSource": "New Mexico WIC publishes federal benefits of up to $50.00 per eligible senior or elder, plus state benefits of up to $100.00 per farmers market season \"upon availability.\" These are maximums, not typical awards. NMDOH's 2026 announcement describes $30 to $50 in benefits, so $50 is the amount actually being issued unless state funds are released. Benefits load onto a Senior Shopper Card for fresh fruits, vegetables, herbs, and honey at participating farmers markets and roadside stands.",
