      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one apply if they're 58 but Native American?",
          "answer": "Yes. Native Americans qualify at age 55, while non-Native Americans must be 60 or older. Make sure to specify tribal membership when applying to ensure the correct age threshold is applied."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if we miss the February application window?",
          "answer": "You can still apply after February, but the program operates first-come, first-served and closes when funding is exhausted. Applications that open in mid-February have the best chance of receiving benefits distributed in July."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can the electronic card be used at regular grocery stores?",
          "answer": "No. The card works only at participating New Mexico farmers markets and roadside stands selling locally grown produce. Farmers must grow within New Mexico or within an 80-mile radius of the state border."
        },
        {
          "question": "If my loved one already gets SNAP, do they need to prove income again?",
          "answer": "No. Participation in SNAP, TEFAP, or CSFP automatically qualifies your loved one for SFMNP without separate income verification. Just provide proof of current participation in those programs."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can we save unused benefits from one year to the next?",
          "answer": "No. The annual $100 benefit expires at the end of each program year, and your loved one must reapply annually. Unused funds don't roll over, so plan to use the full amount during the active benefit period."
        },
        {
          "question": "What exactly can my loved one buy with the card?",
          "answer": "Only fresh, locally grown fruits, vegetables, herbs, and honey from authorized vendors. No processed foods, plants, seeds, or non-food items. Honey is allowed for seniors (unlike the regular WIC program)."
        },
        {
          "question": "How do we find participating farmers markets near us?",
          "answer": "Contact the NM DOH SFMNP office at (505) 469-0548 for a current list of participating markets and vendors. Authorized locations can change seasonally, so verify before making trips."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can I apply on behalf of my elderly loved one?",
          "answer": "Yes, family members can help with the application process. You'll need your loved one's documentation and may need to provide proof of your authority to act on their behalf for some application methods."
        }
      ],
