      "intro": "If your loved one is 55+ and unemployed with family income below 125% of the federal poverty level (roughly $19,000 for a single person, $25,500 for a couple), they may qualify for subsidized part-time training positions through SCSEP. Your loved one would earn wages working typically 20 hours per week at nonprofits or public agencies while building job skills in roles like customer service, teacher assistant, maintenance, or food preparation.\n\nBecause SCSEP pays wages directly and provides on-the-job training, your loved one gains work experience while earning income during their transition back to unsubsidized employment. Priority goes to veterans and their spouses, people 65+, rural residents, those with disabilities, and people with limited English or low literacy skills.\n\nNew Mexico has 26 of 33 counties classified as 'frontier,' so rural residents get priority placement. However, funding fluctuations can create waitlists, and availability varies by region depending on which organization administers the program in your area.",
